<KkShell
  title="Google Ads Agency (Preview)"
  description="Google Ads for businesses that want to catch the people typing it in, in the Dead River look."
  kicker="GOOGLE ADS"
  headline="THEY TYPED IT IN. BE THERE WHEN THEY DO, AND PICK UP IN FIVE MINUTES."
  sub="$50,000 in new revenue in 45-60 days, or your money back, plus $500 for wasting your time."
  video="/50k-demand-flow/assets/demand-flow-vsl-1080p.mp4"
  faqTitle="GOOGLE ADS, STRAIGHT ANSWERS"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p class="kk-dear">Hey,</p>
  <p>If you'd like a simple, honest way to get more customers from Google without lighting your budget on fire, keep reading. This is the one page on the subject I'd want somebody to hand me ten years ago.</p>
  <p>Here's where we're coming from. We've run Google Ads for over 40 businesses in 30 niches across 20 states. An auto shop that went from $20,000 a month to $100,000. A plumber that went from $60,000 a year to $250,000. A freight broker that went from 1 or 2 leads a week to 5 or 6 a day, and one of those leads became a $1.2 million contract.</p>
  <p>And if you've run Google Ads for any length of time, this probably sounds familiar.</p>
  <p>Cost per click keeps climbing. The campaign that was working slows down. You get clicks from three cities over from people who were never going to call. Your agency sends a report full of "impressions."</p>
  <p>So you pull the budget, hire a new guy, and he tells you to redo the logo.</p>
  <p>Here's the good news. It's almost never "Google doesn't work." One step is broken. Here's the three we fix first.</p>
  <h2>STEP ONE: START WITH THE JOB, NOT THE KEYWORD</h2>
  <p>The first thing most people do is grab a pile of search terms and cram them into one ad group. "Plumber El Paso," "plumbing repair," "drain cleaning," "water heater." All in one bucket, one ad, one page.</p>
  <p>That's the fastest way to waste money we know of.</p>
  <p>Pick the job you want more of. Drain clearing. Then one ad group for drain clearing. Then an ad that says "drain clearing." Then a page about drain clearing with a phone number on it. One job, one ad group, one page.</p>
  <p>Google rewards that, because it's exactly what the person searching wanted. You get a higher quality score, cheaper clicks, and a better spot. And every week you check the search terms report and block the junk. "Free water heater" and "water heater jobs" both match "water heater," and neither one is a customer.</p>
  <h2>STEP TWO: THE AD HAS ONE JOB</h2>
  <p>Most ads read like this. "Established in 1998. Biggest selection, best prices. Speak with our team of professionals."</p>
  <p>That's a snoozer. Nobody searched for that.</p>
  <p>They searched for "drain clearing in your city." So the headline says drain clearing in your city. Then one real fact that builds trust, years, license, reviews. Then what happens next. Call, or request a visit. The ad's job is the click. Don't claim "same day" or "free" unless it's true today. Google will approve it. Your customer won't forgive it.</p>
  <h2>STEP THREE: WHERE THE CLICK LANDS</h2>
  <p>Most people send every click to the home page. The person wanted drain clearing and they're looking at a page about everything you do, with a menu and an "about us." They leave.</p>
  <p>Every ad goes to a page about that one thing, with the same words the ad used, a phone number you can hit with a thumb, and a short form. Then the part everybody skips: a person calls that lead back inside five minutes. Not an auto-reply. A person. It's written into our guarantee because it's the biggest reason ads work for one business and not the next.</p>
  <h2>WHERE DEMAND INTELLIGENCE FITS</h2>
  <p>Google tells you who searched. Demand Intelligence tells you who's been looking for days. We upload that list as a customer match audience and bid higher when a search comes from somebody on it. Same words, warmer person.</p>
  <h2>WHY WE PUT IT IN WRITING</h2>
  <p>We know you've tried ads with somebody before. We know you were promised the world. So rather than tell you we're different, we put our own money on it. $50,000 in new revenue in 45-60 days, or you get your money back, plus $500 for wasting your time.</p>
  <p>Your side: keep $3,000 a month in ad spend funded, answer every lead in 5 minutes, log it in the CRM, give it 60 days. That's it.</p>
  <p class="kk-ps">P.S. The three steps above are the first three of a dozen in the Google section of Marketing is F*cking Easy. Free, further down. Read it, run it, or hit the button and we'll run it for you.</p>
</KkShell>
